{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"fel-unfeeling-baiji","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\n\n`acli` (the Atlassian CLI) is a hard runtime prerequisite for rebar's Jira **Cloud** paths, and nothing at install time says so.\n\n- It is invoked as a bare binary resolved from `PATH`: `_DEFAULT_ACLI_CMD: list[str] = [\"acli\"]` (`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli_subprocess.py:32`), used throughout `adapters/jira/acli_cli_ops.py`.\n- It **cannot** be a Python dependency — it is an external binary — so no extra can pull it in.\n- The Jira-related extras declare only Python packages: `jira-datacenter = [\"jira>=3.8,<4\"]` and `adf = [\"marklas\", \"mistune\"]`. There is **no** extra for the Cloud/acli path at all, and no extra mentions the binary.\n- It IS documented in `docs/jira-sync-setup.md` (pinned version + sha256, download URL) — but in a **CI-setup** context (`gh variable set ACLI_VERSION`), not as a prerequisite for someone installing the package.\n\n**Consequence.** A user installs a Jira extra, gets a successful install, and then every Jira-mutating operation fails at runtime with `[Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'acli'`. That errno names a file, not a missing dependency, and gives no install instruction — so the user has no path from the failure to the fix.\n\nObserved 2026-08-28 on the deployed box: with credentials finally reaching the container, `bridge_check_access` returned `{\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"steps\":[{\"step\":\"STEP_CREATE\",\"reason\":\"exception\",\"detail\":\"[Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'acli'\"}]}`. The box case is filed separately as `royalistic-gorgeable-norwayrat` (54e6-ecb0-10c7-451f) — this ticket is the **package-level** sibling: the same gap as it reaches anyone who installs rebar.\n\n## Why it matters for the release\n\nDocumentation and error messages ship. Telling a user to `pip install nava-rebar[...]` for Jira and leaving them to discover an undocumented binary via an errno is a defect in the installed product, not in our deployment.\n\n## Two parts, both wanted\n\n**1. Document it.** State `acli` as a prerequisite wherever the Jira extras are described (extras documentation, install docs, and the packaging metadata description if extras are listed there), with an install pointer. Say plainly WHICH paths need it: the Cloud adapter needs `acli`; the Data Center adapter uses the `jira` Python library and does not.\n\n**2. Fail usefully.** Replace the bare `FileNotFoundError` with a typed, actionable failure at the point of use or in a preflight: name the missing binary, say it is required for Jira Cloud operations, and point at the install docs. `access_check` already fail-closes with typed verdicts (`missing_credentials`, `missing_project`), so a `missing_acli` verdict fits the existing shape rather than inventing one. This is the same principle just landed on `potential-powellite-mouflon` (dbca): a caller-visible failure should carry the class that tells the caller what to do.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The Jira extras' documentation names `acli` as a required external binary for the Cloud path, with an install pointer, and states that the Data Center path does not need it.\n- [ ] A missing `acli` produces a typed, actionable failure naming the binary and the remedy — not a bare `[Errno 2]`.\n- [ ] The typed failure is distinguishable from `missing_credentials` and `missing_project`, so a caller can tell \"not installed\" from \"not configured\".\n- [ ] The Data Center path is demonstrably unaffected — it must not acquire a dependency on `acli`.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Drive the failure with `acli` genuinely absent from `PATH` (manipulate `PATH`, do not monkeypatch the check away) and assert the typed code and message content.\n- **Anchor against vacuity:** a test asserting \"an error was raised\" passes for the WRONG error, which is the defect being fixed. Assert the specific code, and prove the three states are distinguished from each other — missing-binary vs missing-credentials vs missing-project must produce three different results, not merely three failures.\n- Add a positive control: with a stub `acli` on `PATH`, the preflight passes — otherwise the check could be unconditionally failing and the test would not notice.\n- Mutation-check: collapse the new code into the existing ones and confirm the discrimination test fails.","id":"3581-9cf1-d4c0-4082","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"acli is an undeclared, undocumented runtime prerequisite of the Jira extras: install succeeds, then every Cloud path dies with a bare errno"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787964580160932001,"uuid":"a7f161ae-f735-40bf-a83a-a882c871e591"}